{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RjvcsZFXkEJFk1btBWDoSy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read the full paper and the review context. Let me summarize what I know:\n\n**Paper**: \"What Makes Multi-Turn Jailbreaks Detectable? A Fine-Grained Analysis of Lexical, Semantic, and Pragmatic Signals\"\n\nThe paper presents an empirical analysis decomposing multi-turn jailbreak detection across three signal families (lexical, semantic, pragmatic). Key findings:\n1. TF-IDF achieves F1=1.0 IID and 0.94 OOD - lexical artifacts dominate\n2. Vanilla semantic modeling fails OOD (F1=0.28) but persuasion fine-tuning recovers to F1=1.0\n\n\n3. The recovery comes from domain exposure rather than annotation quality—scrambled labels work nearly as well as proper ones, and simpler architectures match more complex ones\n4. LLM judges underperform compared to the learned detectors\n5. Different signal types show varying robustness to perturbations\n\nThe review context describes imagined experiments with expected results and harsh critiques, but the paper has repositioned itself as an empirical analysis rather than a detection method. Now I need to assign reviewers—one for methods and experiments, another for novelty and related work.\n\nI'm setting up a structured review process where both reviewers work independently first, then share findings, identify convergence points, and synthesize their assessments. I'll run them as parallel agents to speed this up.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16084,"cache_read_input_tokens":37133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16084},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"663ec355-7f83-4836-8054-259e8989ba2c","uuid":"bd6cce5a-6286-4ff3-b75b-c6024a7b7224"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RjvcsZFXkEJFk1btBWDoSy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've read the full paper (12 pages) and the review context. Dispatching R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work) in parallel for independent blind reviews."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16084,"cache_read_input_tokens":37133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16084},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"663ec355-7f83-4836-8054-259e8989ba2c","uuid":"b18c26b9-0425-4f3e-bc41-14a963e387e6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RjvcsZFXkEJFk1btBWDoSy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Grwuv8FK8V6fbXReYRMMJB","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper described below independently. Apply top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"What Makes Multi-Turn Jailbreaks Detectable? A Fine-Grained Analysis of Lexical, Semantic, and Pragmatic Signals\"\n\n**Core claim**: Systematic empirical analysis decomposing multi-turn jailbreak detection across lexical, semantic, and pragmatic signal families.\n\n**Data**: 480 training conversations (240 Crescendo jailbreak + 240 topic-anchored benign). OOD evaluation on 80 FITD + 80 Deceptive Delight (DD) synthetic conversations generated by Qwen3-8B. 200 WildChat hard negatives (15% estimated contamination).\n\n**Models compared**:\n- TF-IDF + LR (lexical baseline)\n- Vanilla DeBERTa-v3-base + GRU/mean pooling (semantic baseline)\n- Persuasion-aware DeBERTa (Treatment): fine-tuned on 8-class persuasion strategy classification, multi-task with binary jailbreak-intent head, λ weighting, Bi-GRU or mean pooling aggregation\n- BERT-base + GRU (DeepContext proxy)\n- GPT-5.5 and Qwen3-8B zero-shot (LLM-as-judge)\n\n**Key Results (13 tables)**:\n\nTable 1 - IID Detection: Treatment F1=1.0 at all K, Vanilla baseline K=1 F1=0.968 rising to 1.0 at full, TF-IDF F1=1.0 everywhere. Max gap is 3.2pp at K=1.\n\nTable 2 - TF-IDF across sources: Crescendo IID=1.0, FITD OOD=1.0, DD OOD=0.944, WildChat benign=0.998.\n\nTable 3 - Cross-attack OOD (trained on Crescendo): Treatment=1.0 on DD, Vanilla=0.284 on DD, TF-IDF=0.944 on DD, Scrambled=0.990 on DD. All methods=1.0 on FITD.\n\nTable 4 - Scrambled labels IID: 8-class proper K=1=0.987, scrambled K=1=0.974, baseline K=1=0.968. At full: all=1.0.\n\nTable 5 - Label granularity: 8-class IID=1.0 DD=1.0; Binary IID=1.0 DD=0.990; Baseline IID=1.0 DD=0.284.\n\nTable 6 - Temporal modeling: Treatment+GRU IID=1.0 DD=1.0; Treatment+MeanPool IID=1.0 DD=1.0; Baseline+GRU DD=0.284; Baseline+MeanPool DD=0.295.\n\nTable 7 - Perturbation robustness: Treatment original=1.0 perturbed=1.0 (0pp drop); Vanilla original=1.0 perturbed=0.882 (-11.8pp); TF-IDF original=1.0 perturbed=0.987 (-1.3pp).\n\nTable 8 - LLM-as-judge: GPT-5.5 F1 at K=2=0.19, K=5=0.45, full=0.38, unparseable=30-47%. Upper bound ~0.75. Qwen3-8B full=0.10.\n\nTable 9 - Strategy trajectories: Crescendo uses 8 strategies, mean start=0.86 end=5.64. FITD uses 4. DD uses 6.\n\nTable 10 - WildChat FPR: Treatment=0.12, Vanilla=0.18, TF-IDF=0.08.\n\nTable 11 - Lambda ablation: All values (0, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0) produce F1=1.0 on both IID and DD.\n\nTable 12 - Checkpoint variance: Epoch 2/3/Final all IID=1.0, DD ranges 0.98-1.0.\n\nTable 13 - Encoder comparison: BERT+GRU IID=0.95 DD=0.35; DeBERTa vanilla IID=1.0 DD=0.284; DeBERTa FT IID=1.0 DD=1.0.\n\n**Limitations acknowledged**: Synthetic OOD data, small scale (480 train, 160 OOD), missing non-persuasion fine-tuning controls, weak perturbation, unoptimized LLM prompts, WildChat contamination.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions in depth. Be rigorous and specific, citing table numbers and specific results:\n\n1. **Experimental methodology**: Is the experimental design sound? Are controls adequate? Are there confounds?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are results statistically meaningful given sample sizes and variance?\n3. **Baseline adequacy**: Are the baselines appropriate and fairly compared?\n4. **Ablation completeness**: Do the ablations isolate the claimed factors? What's missing?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Could the experiments be reproduced?\n6. **Claims vs evidence alignment**: Does each claim have adequate experimental support?\n\n## Output Format\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (numbered list, be specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (numbered list, be specific with severity: minor/moderate/major/fatal)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing Experiments** (what would strengthen the paper)\n- **Detailed Comments** (section-by-section if needed)\n- **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n- **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16084,"cache_read_input_tokens":37133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16084},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"663ec355-7f83-4836-8054-259e8989ba2c","uuid":"206e062b-290c-428c-90ed-8b9cc11085e4"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a69249844f751efb4","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Grwuv8FK8V6fbXReYRMMJB","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper described below independently. Apply top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"What Makes Multi-Turn Jailbreaks Detectable? A Fine-Grained Analysis of Lexical, Semantic, and Pragmatic Signals\"\n\n**Core claim**: Systematic empirical analysis decomposing multi-turn jailbreak detection across lexical, semantic, and pragmatic signal families.\n\n**Data**: 480 training conversations (240 Crescendo jailbreak + 240 topic-anchored benign). OOD evaluation on 80 FITD + 80 Deceptive Delight (DD) synthetic conversations generated by Qwen3-8B. 200 WildChat hard negatives (15% estimated contamination).\n\n**Models compared**:\n- TF-IDF + LR (lexical baseline)\n- Vanilla DeBERTa-v3-base + GRU/mean pooling (semantic baseline)\n- Persuasion-aware DeBERTa (Treatment): fine-tuned on 8-class persuasion strategy classification, multi-task with binary jailbreak-intent head, λ weighting, Bi-GRU or mean pooling aggregation\n- BERT-base + GRU (DeepContext proxy)\n- GPT-5.5 and Qwen3-8B zero-shot (LLM-as-judge)\n\n**Key Results (13 tables)**:\n\nTable 1 - IID Detection: Treatment F1=1.0 at all K, Vanilla baseline K=1 F1=0.968 rising to 1.0 at full, TF-IDF F1=1.0 everywhere. Max gap is 3.2pp at K=1.\n\nTable 2 - TF-IDF across sources: Crescendo IID=1.0, FITD OOD=1.0, DD OOD=0.944, WildChat benign=0.998.\n\nTable 3 - Cross-attack OOD (trained on Crescendo): Treatment=1.0 on DD, Vanilla=0.284 on DD, TF-IDF=0.944 on DD, Scrambled=0.990 on DD. All methods=1.0 on FITD.\n\nTable 4 - Scrambled labels IID: 8-class proper K=1=0.987, scrambled K=1=0.974, baseline K=1=0.968. At full: all=1.0.\n\nTable 5 - Label granularity: 8-class IID=1.0 DD=1.0; Binary IID=1.0 DD=0.990; Baseline IID=1.0 DD=0.284.\n\nTable 6 - Temporal modeling: Treatment+GRU IID=1.0 DD=1.0; Treatment+MeanPool IID=1.0 DD=1.0; Baseline+GRU DD=0.284; Baseline+MeanPool DD=0.295.\n\nTable 7 - Perturbation robustness: Treatment original=1.0 perturbed=1.0 (0pp drop); Vanilla original=1.0 perturbed=0.882 (-11.8pp); TF-IDF original=1.0 perturbed=0.987 (-1.3pp).\n\nTable 8 - LLM-as-judge: GPT-5.5 F1 at K=2=0.19, K=5=0.45, full=0.38, unparseable=30-47%. Upper bound ~0.75. Qwen3-8B full=0.10.\n\nTable 9 - Strategy trajectories: Crescendo uses 8 strategies, mean start=0.86 end=5.64. FITD uses 4. DD uses 6.\n\nTable 10 - WildChat FPR: Treatment=0.12, Vanilla=0.18, TF-IDF=0.08.\n\nTable 11 - Lambda ablation: All values (0, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0) produce F1=1.0 on both IID and DD.\n\nTable 12 - Checkpoint variance: Epoch 2/3/Final all IID=1.0, DD ranges 0.98-1.0.\n\nTable 13 - Encoder comparison: BERT+GRU IID=0.95 DD=0.35; DeBERTa vanilla IID=1.0 DD=0.284; DeBERTa FT IID=1.0 DD=1.0.\n\n**Limitations acknowledged**: Synthetic OOD data, small scale (480 train, 160 OOD), missing non-persuasion fine-tuning controls, weak perturbation, unoptimized LLM prompts, WildChat contamination.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions in depth. Be rigorous and specific, citing table numbers and specific results:\n\n1. **Experimental methodology**: Is the experimental design sound? Are controls adequate? Are there confounds?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are results statistically meaningful given sample sizes and variance?\n3. **Baseline adequacy**: Are the baselines appropriate and fairly compared?\n4. **Ablation completeness**: Do the ablations isolate the claimed factors? What's missing?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Could the experiments be reproduced?\n6. **Claims vs evidence alignment**: Does each claim have adequate experimental support?\n\n## Output Format\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (numbered list, be specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (numbered list, be specific with severity: minor/moderate/major/fatal)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing Experiments** (what would strengthen the paper)\n- **Detailed Comments** (section-by-section if needed)\n- **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n- **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n","uuid":"34d9583a-37b8-40d2-b9b2-0d98de125d50","session_id":"663ec355-7f83-4836-8054-259e8989ba2c"}
